WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.435
Detections are not one-time creations.

2
00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:07.593
Systems change, technologies evolve, threats adapt, and organizations mature.

3
00:00:07.593 --> 00:00:14.109
A detection that was useful last year may become noisy, stale, or incomplete as conditions change.

4
00:00:14.109 --> 00:00:23.308
The detection lifecycle includes design, implementation, testing, deployment, monitoring, tuning, documentation, and retirement.

5
00:00:23.308 --> 00:00:30.233
Each stage should preserve context so future maintainers understand the purpose and assumptions behind the detection.

6
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:33.106
Maintenance should be based on feedback and evidence.

7
00:00:33.106 --> 00:00:44.461
Alert outcomes, analyst comments, incident lessons, telemetry changes, and business context can all show whether a detection needs tuning, enrichment, suppression, or replacement.

8
00:00:44.461 --> 00:00:47.315
A sustainable program makes review routine.

9
00:00:47.315 --> 00:00:55.925
Detections should have owners, review dates, clear logic, expected outputs, and criteria for when they should be improved or retired.
